------------+-----------------------------------
      Total |     91,770      100.00

.         
.         * Step 1: Create year indicators for first 1 and first 0
.         bysort pid (syear): egen first_1year = min(cond(d_daughter == 1, syea
> r, .))
(330,882 missing values generated)

.         bysort pid (syear): egen first_0year = min(cond(d_daughter == 0, syea
> r, .))
(268,926 missing values generated)

. 
.         * Step 2: Count total non-missing observations per id
.         bysort pid: egen total_obs = total(!missing(d_daughter))

. 
.         * Step 3: Generate new variable
.         gen d_daughter_filled = .
(573,844 missing values generated)

.         * Rule 1: Leave missing if always missing
.         replace d_daughter_filled = . if total_obs == 0
(0 real changes made)

.         * Rule 2: Years before first 1 are 0 if a 0 ever appeared
.         replace d_daughter_filled = 0 if syear < first_1year & !missing(first
> _0year) & total_obs > 0
(260,589 real changes made)

.         * Rule 3: Years from first 1 onward are 1
.         replace d_daughter_filled = 1 if syear >= first_1year & total_obs > 0
(186,767 real changes made)

.         * Rule 4: If no 1 exists but 0 exists, set all years after 0 to 0
.         replace d_daughter_filled = 0 if missing(first_1year) & syear >= firs
> t_0year & total_obs > 0
(0 real changes made)

. 
.         * Step 4: Clean up
.         drop first_1year first_0year total_obs  

.         drop d_daughter

.         rename d_daughter_filled d_daughter

.         
.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * 
. 
.         * This analysis tests the hypothesis that having daughters leads to s
> tronger support for gender equality (as measured by attitudes).
.         
.         * The dependent variable is plh0301, which captures agreement with th
> e statement: “Women should rather care about family than career.” The variabl
> e is reverse-coded, such that higher values indicate stronger disagreement wi
> th the statement and thus greater support for women’s career rights.
. 
.         * The key independent variable is d_daughter, a dummy equal to 1 if t
> he respondent has at least one daughter, and 0 otherwise.
. 
.         * The regression is estimated using a linear OLS model, with the samp
> le restricted to the 2012 wave, which is the only year that includes both the
>  dependent variable and the daughter indicator.
. 
.         * Control variables include respondent gender (sex), year of birth (g
> ebjahr), marital status (pgfamstd), and years of education (pgbilzeit), to ac
> count for demographic and socioeconomic differences in gender attitudes.
. 
.         * Expected finding: If having daughters increases support for gender 
> equality, we expect a positive and statistically significant coefficient on d
> _daughter, indicating that parents with daughters are more likely to reject t
> raditional gender norms about women’s role in the labor market.
. 
.         * H1:
.         reg plh0301 d_daughter sex gebjahr i.pgfamstd pgbilzeit if (plh0301 !
> = -8) & (plh0301 != -5) & (plh0301 != -1) & (pgfamstd != -3) & (pgfamstd != -
> 1) & (pgbilzeit != -2) & (pgbilzeit != -1)

